casebook piper-permit v1
source "Public Inquiry into the Piper Alpha Disaster (Cullen, 1990); Energy Institute incident summary" dated 1988-07-06 by "Piper Alpha, North Sea"
note "167 fatalities, 62 survivors. Standby condensate pump A was de-energised for maintenance; its discharge"
note "pressure safety valve had been REMOVED and blind flange assemblies fitted to the open PSV connections."
note "A permit-to-work had been raised and suspended, sitting in the permit box -- the formal signal that pump A"
note "must not be started. The running condensate pump B then failed and would not restart, separator liquid"
note "levels began rising toward a total platform shutdown, and the night shift -- who knew pump A had been taken"
note "out of service but BELIEVED THE WORK HAD NOT YET BEGUN -- re-energised and started it."
note "Cullen: a breakdown in communication at shift handover, and failure of the permit-to-work system."
note ""
note "This casebook exists to test a specific claim quorum makes. The night shift's belief was an inference from"
note "the ABSENCE of information: nobody had told them the work had started. Under closed-world reasoning an"
note "absent observation is decisively false, which is exactly how 'no one mentioned it' becomes 'it did not"
note "happen'. The witness is required to render that step as an ASSUMPTION, in capitals. If it does not, the"
note "product does not do what it claims."
note ""
note "A third problem shape, deliberately: the pump casebook weighs competing hypotheses and the tank casebook"
note "weighs disagreeing sources. This one reasons about RECORDS AND AUTHORITY, where the evidence is a document"
note "somebody did or did not consult rather than anything an instrument can report."

# ─── positive acts: things somebody actually did ────────────────────────────
# Every one of these is an ACT, not a state of the world, and that is the point. A control room can know that a
# walkdown happened; it cannot know that no maintenance is in progress.
observe psv_certified      "the PSV has been reinstalled and certified, and that certificate is in hand"
observe walkdown_done      "this shift has physically inspected pump A and its pipework"
observe handover_covered   "the outgoing shift explicitly briefed this shift on pump A"
observe permit_suspended   "a SUSPENDED permit-to-work for pump A has been found and read"
observe permit_cleared     "a CLEARED permit-to-work for pump A has been found and read"
observe blind_flange_seen  "a blind flange assembly has been observed on the PSV connections"

# ─── the production situation ───────────────────────────────────────────────
observe pump_b_failed      "the running condensate pump has failed and will not restart"
observe level_high         "separator liquid level rising toward a total platform shutdown"

channel separator_level "gas/liquid separator level" unit percent
  range 0 100
  stale_after 5s
  flatline_after 10m epsilon 0.3
  asserts level_high above 80.0 release 70.0

# cbc warns that nothing reasons about `separator_level_unavailable` or `separator_level_flatline`. Both warnings are
# left standing with their reasoning recorded rather than silenced.
#
# A separator transmitter that is dead or stuck can only mislead THIS casebook in one direction. `level_high` either
# sticks on, which asserts production pressure and routes the decision to a person, or is absent, which removes the
# production argument entirely and leaves the pump isolated. An urgent restart cannot be justified by a level nobody
# can measure, and declining to justify it is the conservative answer.
#
# That is a property of this casebook's structure and not a general excuse. In the tank casebook a stuck level
# transmitter IS the accident, which is why the identical warning is answered there by an explicit doubt that
# discards the reading.

# ─── the argument that was never made on the night ──────────────────────────
# This is the whole casebook. It holds by default, and it is defeated ONLY by a positive act -- never by the
# absence of a warning. An author who instead wrote "unknown unless a suspended permit was found" would have
# encoded the night shift's reasoning exactly, and the compiler could not tell the difference, because that
# casebook is well formed. What the machine CAN do is make the assumption visible in every witness it prints.
claim work_status_unknown "nobody on this shift has established what was done to pump A"

psv_certified    rebuts work_status_unknown
walkdown_done    rebuts work_status_unknown
handover_covered rebuts work_status_unknown

# Positive evidence that the pressure boundary is open. Either of these alone is enough.
claim boundary_open "pump A may have an open pressure boundary"
  when any blind_flange_seen permit_suspended

# Positive evidence that it is closed. Note this requires the CERTIFICATE, not merely a cleared permit: a permit
# records that work was signed off, a certificate records that the valve is back and tested.
claim boundary_closed "the pressure boundary on pump A is intact"
  needs psv_certified

boundary_open   rebuts boundary_closed
permit_suspended rebuts boundary_closed

# The hazard. Starting a pump whose pressure boundary nobody has confirmed.
claim unverified_start "starting a pump whose pressure boundary has not been verified"
  when any work_status_unknown boundary_open

# ─── the pressure to start it anyway ───────────────────────────────────────
# This argument is included because it is real, and because a casebook that omits it cannot explain why a competent
# crew started the pump. Leaving it out would make the machine look wiser than the people it is meant to help.
claim production_at_risk "the platform will trip if liquid levels are not brought down"
  needs level_high

claim restart_needed "the standby pump is the available means of reducing level"
  needs production_at_risk pump_b_failed

# ─── what to do ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The correct action is not "refuse". It is to route the decision to the authority that can weigh a production trip
# against an unverified pressure boundary -- which is a person, not a control room, and certainly not this machine.
#
# Note `when all` on start_pump_a. The first draft of this file said `when boundary_closed restart_needed`, meaning
# "both", and a bare `when` list is a DISJUNCTION -- so it licensed starting the pump on production pressure alone,
# with no certificate that the pressure boundary was closed. That is the Piper Alpha decision exactly, reproduced by
# accident, in the casebook written to prevent it. The compiler accepted it because it was well formed; the
# exhaustive invariant below is what caught it. `when` with several conditions now requires `any` or `all`.
action monitor          idle
action start_pump_a     when all boundary_closed restart_needed
action escalate_to_oim  when all unverified_start restart_needed
action keep_isolated    safe when unverified_start

# Declared precedence, and this is the line that matters. On the night it lived in nobody's head in any explicit
# form: a production imperative was weighed against a maintenance status nobody had checked, in a few minutes, at
# shift handover. Here it is a reviewable line in a versioned document.
#
# `escalate_to_oim` outranks `keep_isolated` because escalating does not start anything -- the pump stays isolated
# either way, and the difference is whether a person who can authorise the trip is told. Staying quietly isolated
# while the platform drifts toward a shutdown nobody has been warned about is not the safer option; it is the same
# information failure in the opposite direction.
prefer escalate_to_oim over keep_isolated over start_pump_a over monitor
